{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/fetcher.py` measures **771 lines** against the hard 800-line\nmodule-size gate (`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), which CI enforces with no allowlist. The sibling\nstory that fans the fetch out over several projects rewrites the query construction in\n`_build_snapshot` and will not fit in 29 lines.\n\n## Approach\n\nExtract the pagination cluster into a new `fetch_paging.py`: `_ACLI_CEILING` and\n`SilentTruncationError` (`:99-120`), `_extract_issues` (`:251-262`), `_iter_pages` (`:265-390`) and\n`collect` (`:392-399`).\n\nThe call chain is genuine and self-contained: `collect` calls `_iter_pages` (`:397`), which calls\n`_extract_issues` (`:296`) and raises `SilentTruncationError` (`:308,343,355`) against\n`_ACLI_CEILING` (`:354`). Nothing in the cluster calls back upward; `_build_snapshot` reaches it only\nthrough `_iter_pages` (`:554`). The new module's complete import list is `annotations` and a\n`TYPE_CHECKING` import of `TicketTransport` — the pagination code is otherwise pure.\n\nThis extraction is well matched to the incoming change, which rewrites the `queries` tuple at\n`_build_snapshot:548-554` into a per-project loop over `jqls_for` — all of which stays in `fetcher.py`.\n\n**Three things must stay resident**, and getting any of them wrong breaks tests silently:\n\n- `_DONE_RECENT_CAP` (`:105`) stays — `_build_snapshot:550` uses it. Only `_ACLI_CEILING` moves.\n- `_load_acli` and `_load_alert_store` stay — they carry **25** and **3** `patch.object(fetcher, ...)`\n  sites respectively, and a module-attribute patch does not survive a module move. Both are called\n  from `_build_snapshot` (`:523,536`), which stays, so no patch site is disturbed.\n- `fetcher.py` re-exports **all four** moved names — `_iter_pages`, `collect`,\n  `SilentTruncationError` and `_ACLI_CEILING` — because every one of them is reached as an attribute\n  of `fetcher` by existing tests, and an attribute access does not follow a moved symbol:\n  - `hasattr(fetcher, \"_iter_pages\")` and `hasattr(fetcher, \"collect\")`\n    (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/fetch/test_fetcher_snapshot.py:118,139`), plus further\n    `fetcher._iter_pages` / `fetcher.collect` accesses in the same file and in\n    `test_fetcher_pagination_heldout.py`;\n  - `fetcher.SilentTruncationError`\n    (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/fetch/test_fetcher_pagination_jql.py:133,165`) as well as the\n    explicit `from rebar_reconciler.fetcher import SilentTruncationError` at\n    `test_fetcher_pagination_heldout.py:32`;\n  - `fetcher._ACLI_CEILING` (`test_fetcher_pagination_heldout.py:149`), which reads the constant\n    directly to size its fixture. This one is easy to miss precisely because the constant looks\n    private and unshared.\n\nThe re-export keeps roughly 48 existing references green across six test modules. Note that the two\nproduction mentions of `fetcher._iter_pages` — `_backend.py:62` and\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/_base.py:139,153` — are **docstring prose** recording bug history, not\nruntime references, so no production module needs an edit.\n\nThis is a behaviour-preserving move: no logic is rewritten.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/fetch_paging.py` (new) — the moved cluster, ~195 lines\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/fetcher.py` — drops to ~606 lines, plus a re-export block\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new behaviour, so the oracle is the existing suite passing unchanged. The risks are attribute\npresence and patch survival, so both are asserted rather than assumed:\n\n- Attribute-surface assertion: `hasattr(fetcher, \"_iter_pages\")` and `hasattr(fetcher, \"collect\")`\n  both hold after the move, and `SilentTruncationError` still imports from `fetcher`.\n- Patch survival: the tests that `patch.object(fetcher, \"_load_acli\")` and `\"_load_alert_store\"` pass\n  unedited, proving those symbols stayed resident and are still resolved from `fetcher`'s globals.\n- Truncation behaviour preserved: a fixture exceeding `_ACLI_CEILING` still raises\n  `SilentTruncationError` through the real `collect → _iter_pages` path — the contract most at risk\n  from moving the constant away from its raiser.\n- One-way import: `fetch_paging` must not import `fetcher`, asserted directly.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `fetcher.py` and `fetch_paging.py` are each at or below 650 lines; checked by the module-size\n      gate with both counts recorded.\n- [ ] All four moved names — `_iter_pages`, `collect`, `SilentTruncationError` and `_ACLI_CEILING` —\n      still resolve as attributes of `fetcher`; checked by a test asserting each by attribute access,\n      which covers the two existing `hasattr` assertions and the direct `fetcher._ACLI_CEILING` read.\n- [ ] `_load_acli`, `_load_alert_store` and `_DONE_RECENT_CAP` remain defined in `fetcher.py`;\n      checked by asserting each is in that module's own namespace, not merely importable.\n- [ ] A fixture exceeding the ceiling still raises `SilentTruncationError` via the real paging path;\n      checked by the existing pagination held-out test running unedited.\n- [ ] The full existing test suite passes with no test-file edits; checked by the gating test command."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786162949041884001,"uuid":"314afe83-0c4f-47d8-b44b-78544c8487ef"}